            <ArticleTitle>Favorable impact of stents after emergent coronary artery bypass for failed angioplasty.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1644-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">This study was undertaken to determine the impact of the use and availability of coronary stents on outcomes in patients requiring emergent coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery following a failed percutaneous transluminal coronary angioplasty (PTCA).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Patients were divided into two groups based on the year of their CABG for a failed PTCA and the availability of stents: group 1, 1992 to 1994, stents not available (n = 34); and group 2, 1995 to 1997, stents available (n = 26).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">CABG patients in the group where stents were not available were more likely to have had an abrupt coronary occlusion (26 of 34 versus 3 of 26; p &lt; 0.0001) and less likely to have had a dissection (8 of 34 versus 23 of 26; p &lt; 0.0001) as their indication for emergent CABG. Patients in the stent era had a lower incidence of perioperative myocardial infarction (5 of 26 versus 17 of 34; p &lt; 0.01) and a decreased mortality rate (0 of 26 versus 6 of 34; p &lt; 0.03). In the 9 patients where stents were employed, patency of the lumen was restored in 8 patients and there was only 1 myocardial infarction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Stents have had a favorable impact on patients requiring an emergent CABG following a failed PTCA.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
